to big time somebody.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.980
I promise you, every time
somebody says Gary Vee,

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:14.670
I'm so grateful.

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:15.933
I'm so flattered.

00:17:17.210 --> 00:17:18.043
You know?

00:17:18.043 --> 00:17:19.730
And so I will never take that for granted

00:17:19.730 --> 00:17:22.016
that somebody appreciates my work.

00:17:22.016 --> 00:17:24.500
I will never, I will go to the grave.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:26.760
And by the way, on the record,

00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.270
I don't even think I've started yet.

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:30.580
- I love that statement.

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:32.110
- I really am telling you the truth.

00:17:32.110 --> 00:17:35.240
Like, as you know, 10 years ago
I started making some juice.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:36.720
I'd already built a big
business for my dad,

00:17:36.720 --> 00:17:38.820
so I had some credibility.

00:17:38.820 --> 00:17:41.520
And I mean, I'm in a totally
different stratosphere

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:43.060
than I was back then.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.010
And that's exactly how I feel right now

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.240
of who I'm going to be at 54

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:47.937
because I think, I have good intent.

00:17:47.937 --> 00:17:49.940
I believe in what doing.

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:51.650
I'm working hard.

00:17:51.650 --> 00:17:53.190
I'm working fair.

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:56.840
I'm doing the things that,
you know, lead to good things

00:17:56.840 --> 00:17:59.230
- Gary, what is it that
really matters to you?

00:17:59.230 --> 00:18:01.967
Like what is it that you wake
up every day excited to do

00:18:01.967 --> 00:18:03.403
and to give to the world?

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:09.210
- I have this weird blend
of guilt and gratitude

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:12.300
that I was gifted circumstances

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.040
and an ability to communicate.

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.240
And I want to leave that.

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:17.310
I want to leave that.

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:20.413
I want that legacy, selfishly.

00:18:23.325 --> 00:18:25.997
I'm addicted to admiration, right?

00:18:27.050 --> 00:18:29.440
And I think that
admiration is only earned.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:32.560
You don't, you know,
admiration doesn't come...

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:33.393
let me rephrase.

00:18:33.393 --> 00:18:35.030
My definition of admiration is,

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:38.190
you don't get admiration
unless you really do something

00:18:38.190 --> 00:18:40.270
really meaningful, deep.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:45.090
Like, I'm amazed by athletes.

00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:48.950
I'm amazed by certain
people's general beauty.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:53.950
I'm amazed by people's
abilities to build companies

00:18:54.090 --> 00:18:57.610
or incredible art.

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.740
But for me to admire someone,

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:01.640
that means they've done something

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.640
that really touched my soul.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:07.230
Like, they're incredibly just,

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:11.640
kind and generous.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:16.640
And their humanity is on
a scale above the rest.

00:19:17.610 --> 00:19:20.710
And I'm very focused on
trying to create that.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:24.200
Now, that comes at the contrast
of being a businessman.

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:27.200
That's not where the world
is looking at businesses,

00:19:27.200 --> 00:19:29.000
but it is my truth.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:31.370
I love doing business.

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:33.550
And I love nice.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:34.540
I'm addicted to nice.

00:19:34.540 --> 00:19:38.800
So I want to leave a legacy
where people feel compelled

00:19:38.800 --> 00:19:40.460
to come to my funeral one day,

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:43.640
or tell their daughter a story,

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.550
or whatever the social
network of the day is,

00:19:46.550 --> 00:19:50.098
or however we communicate, you know,

00:19:50.098 --> 00:19:52.190
to say something nice.

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:53.350
I want to do that.

00:19:53.350 --> 00:19:54.410
I want to live that life.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:57.020
That is super interesting to me.

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:58.070
- That's beautiful.

00:19:58.070 --> 00:19:59.180
Beautiful. I love that.

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:01.260
And you definitely resonate kindness.

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:03.960
I've noticed that about
you, a number of times.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:05.300
So here's a question from Adam.

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:07.100
- Please.
- Adam is asking,

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:08.915
I've been following you for two years now.

00:20:08.915 --> 00:20:12.340
Wonderful to to see
you here on Mindvalley.

00:20:12.340 --> 00:20:14.140
What advice do you have for people

00:20:14.140 --> 00:20:16.763
starting a new career midlife?

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:19.840
- Contextualize that you're early.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:22.370
I think a lot of people,
when they think midlife,

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:24.110
don't realize how early that is,

00:20:24.110 --> 00:20:28.330
and they feel this pressure
that they need this to work.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.230
Once you take the pressure out,

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:32.730
that you might make this
switch, and it might not work,

00:20:32.730 --> 00:20:34.560
which you can then go
back to the old thing,

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.200
or try a third thing,

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:38.250
if you could eliminate that pressure

00:20:39.490 --> 00:20:41.800
because it's about calibrating time.

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:43.763
If you have a good relationship
with time and realize,

00:20:43.763 --> 00:20:47.740
how old is he, if you could, was it Adam?

00:20:47.740 --> 00:20:48.600
I'm sorry, I didn't...

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:49.870
- Yeah Adam, how old are you?

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:51.040
Post it in the comments.

00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:52.960
- You know, I want to see it for context.

00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.500
And maybe it's not even for him.

00:20:54.500 --> 00:20:55.840
Adam, if it's not for you,

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:57.340
if it's for your mom or
dad or somebody else,

00:20:57.340 --> 00:20:59.300
put the number of that age.

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:00.770
- 41.
- Yeah. And Adam,

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:03.240
is it for you? You can just
say yes or what have you.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:08.240
You know, like, to me, if
it's for Adam, let's say,

00:21:08.330 --> 00:21:11.683
at 41 years old, he's uncomfortably young.

00:21:13.020 --> 00:21:13.853
- Mm.

00:21:13.853 --> 00:21:15.950
- There's a really good
chance, on a conservative day,

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:18.950
he's got 25 years of
profession in front of him,

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:22.080
which is more time than
he's already put in

00:21:22.080 --> 00:21:24.620
since he was 18 to 22.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:25.970
Think about that.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:27.930
But that's not what we do, my friend.

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:28.763
We don't do that.

00:21:28.763 --> 00:21:32.380
We do not do a good job in
having a relationship with time.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:33.830
We think at 41 we're old,

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:35.980
'cause society has done a very bad job

00:21:35.980 --> 00:21:37.835
of creating time.

00:21:37.835 --> 00:21:40.750
'Cause we're going off of old norms

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:42.890
where people did live to 50 or 60.

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:44.913
But we're living to 80, 90 and 100.

00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:48.480
And because of our health
and wellness initiatives,

00:21:48.480 --> 00:21:51.300
as a global society, we at 41,

00:21:51.300 --> 00:21:53.490
and mentally the
youthification of our society,

00:21:53.490 --> 00:21:56.520
all of a sudden 41 is really 25,

00:21:56.520 --> 00:21:57.943
but nobody feels that.

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.720
And so, that's my biggest advice

00:22:01.720 --> 00:22:03.340
which is take the pressure out.

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:06.080
You know, when I see Amber at 34 in here.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:08.630
Like, fuck man, I look
at 34 and I'm like, ugh!

00:22:08.630 --> 00:22:10.320
By the way, I restarted my life at 34.

00:22:10.320 --> 00:22:13.610
At 34, I had spent my
entire life, 12 years,

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:15.770
building a business for my
dad, in a family business.

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:17.250
I didn't get paid what I was worth.

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:19.270
I built a business from
three to 65 million.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:21.220
I was never making any real money at all

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:22.320
because it was a family business.

00:22:22.320 --> 00:22:24.430
You know, you inherit
it when your dad dies.

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:25.350
I leave at 34.

00:22:25.350 --> 00:22:26.210
I own nothing.

00:22:26.210 --> 00:22:27.620
I don't own the Wine Library.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:31.230
I have no money on
paper to borrow it even.

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:33.530
But I fucking built it.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:36.160
So I've had this huge
12 year accomplishment.

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:40.320
And what I get for it, is
to start over with zero.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:43.010
And I'm thrilled because I
accomplished what I wanted

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:46.490
which is, I wanted to do
something great for my parents.

00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:47.970
That took humility and patience.

00:22:47.970 --> 00:22:51.440
I'm at 34, I start VaynerMedia
in the conference room

00:22:51.440 --> 00:22:53.240
of Buddy Media, 'cause
I don't have any money

00:22:53.240 --> 00:22:54.393
for us to pay rent.

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:57.860
- I did not know that.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:00.393
- So I started over at 34.

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:03.460
- Wow. And VaynerMedia,
what year was that?

00:23:03.460 --> 00:23:04.600
That must have been 2009, 2010, right?

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:06.538
- That's right, I was born in '75.

00:23:06.538 --> 00:23:09.707
- Wow, wow. You're about
a year older than me.

00:23:09.707 --> 00:23:11.333
- You know, and so,

00:23:11.333 --> 00:23:14.850
this goes back to excuses
and accountability.

00:23:14.850 --> 00:23:15.707
Sometimes I meet people that are like,

